Rules:
- Use only the map data and the sources in the messages, never what you know.
- The object's own map data is about it. Any other source is about this object only if it
  names one of the object's addresses, or a range of house numbers on the same street that
  includes one, or the object's name for a site at this place. Spellings such as "Str." and
  "Straße", or "1a" and "1 a", are the same address. A source about another address, even next
  door, or about a site of the same name elsewhere, is not about this object, and nothing it
  says is a fact about this object.
- A map tag such as landuse=industrial says only that the land is industrial: on its own, it
  does not settle the type.
- Code checks that each key fact appears word for word in a cited source, as one unbroken
  passage, and rejects the whole finding if one does not. A business's name, its address and
  what it does often stand on separate lines, with other lines between them, as in a
  directory entry or an Impressum. Quote such lines as separate key facts, such as "Muster
  GmbH" and "Beispielstraße 5", never as one, such as "Muster GmbH Beispielstraße 5 10115
  Berlin" when other lines stand between them. Add nothing of your own to a quote: no "...",
  no colon or comma that joins two quotes, no heading or label.
- If no source about this object settles the type, the outcome is unidentified, and the
  identified type and the status are null. If the sources about this object settle the type
  but not the status, the status is null.
- The status is about today. A current listing of a business at the object's address, such
  as a directory entry or its own website, is enough for operating, unless a source about
  this object says that it closed or moved. A document from years ago that shows the site in
  use, such as a report, a register or a permit, can settle the type, but not that the site is
  in use today: on its own, it leaves the status null. Closed needs a source that says so.
